            </HouseholdOperatingConditions>
            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="2" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="2" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

There are a few key locations where excessive air leakage exists in your home. These include from the exterior door in the kitchen that does not seal well, around many of the older wood windows, around the 2 attic hatches and coming up from the crawlspace into the main floor of the original (central) part of the house.



By far, the most intriguing location is from the crawlspace. We could see air movement from around the 3 stairs going up into the kitchen, and coming up through the heat ducts themselves. As there was no access to see inside that crawlspace, we could not see if the ductwork is insulated (as it passes through the cold crawlspace), if indeed the floor above that crawlspace is insulated (I assumed this was the case, and the crawlspace is unheated - ie: no heat ducts pushing heat into that location). I had emailed you a CMHC "Garbage Bag" test that you can perform to test the air flow coming out of each of the ducts in that main floor living space, and compare it to what is coming out of other ducts (ie: the one above the water tank).



I have modeled the ability for you to reduce air leakage in your house by a minimum 10%, although I fully expect you with achieve 20%+ if you can address the air leakage from the crawlspace.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 233&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;9.4 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. If you achieve a 20% reduction in air leakage, the annual savings will double &amp; the rebate would increase to $600.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a second home energy evaluation, where the air leakage reduction will be measured. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of most sections of this house are 2x6 R20, including the wood foundation walls of the basement. There are two remaining section of wall on the main floor of the original house that are 10" log construction, with an interior insulated stud wall constructed on the interior. As discussed for your future renovation planning, I have modeled adding an additional R10 breathable exterior rigid insulation. It is recommended to have the exterior rigid insulation represent at least 50% of the R-value of the insulation located within the stud wall.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By increasing 100% of the exterior walls (including the above grade portion of the 2x6 R20 basement walls) with an additional R10, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 426&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;17.3 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,875 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



</mainWalls>
                <windows>

Overall, the current wood windows are in average condition, however some are really starting to show signs of wear &amp; tear .......... and no longer closing well. For your future renovation planning (together with adding exterior wall insulation - also outlined within this report), I have modeled an upgrade for all windows to ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows.





&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing the windows with ENERGY STARÂ® qualified units rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 542&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;22.0GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</windows>
                <doors>

I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading the 2 current entrance doors to ENERGY STARÂ® qualified polyurethane insulation filled doors rated for climate zone 3.





&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing the 2 current entrance doors with ENERGY STARÂ® qualified units rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 10&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;0.4 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</doors>
                <HotWaterSystem>

Your house has a 40 USG power vented propane hot water tank - this tank has an estimated energy factor of 0.71, which means about 29% of the energy going into this tank is either wasted out through the power vented sidewall chimney or given off in excess heat into the house (which isn't great in summer). As that tank is quite new, I am just modeling (for your information) the expected energy &amp; cost savings if you had an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous, on-demand condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your hot water tank with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous, on demand condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 91/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;4.0 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,250 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem />
                <Renewables />
                <WaterConservation />
                <AdditionalComments>

&lt;b&gt;Crawlspace Issues&lt;/b&gt;



You will see in many other area of this report, I talk about issues with the crawlspace below the main central part of the house. To summarize, the issues I raise are about:



1. There is a large section of the heat supply ductwork entering that crawlspace from the adjoining basement (where the furnace is located), and there is no access to see inside that crawlspace.

2. I have assumed the crawlspace is unheated space. This means that I expect the ductwork inside the crawlspace to have joints sealed and all of the ductwork is fully insulated. I would expect that ductwork not to have any heat supply opening into the crawlspace. I would also expect the floor above the crawlspace (the floor you walk on) to be insulated.

3.There was alot of air leakage coming from that crawlspace, as noticed from around the 3 stairs going into the kitchen, and coming up through the ductwork into the living room. Perhaps some ductwork in the crawlspace has fallen apart?

4. I have emailed you the "garbage bag" test so you can check the flow from each of the heating ducts entering the house - there may be various rooms where the heat ducts have come apart?



Ideally, you would like the heat to be coming into the house &amp; not the crawlspace. You will therefore need to have someone fully explore that crawlspace to ensure that all heating ducts are connected, sealed and insulated. While in there, it is a great opportunity to air seal the crawlspace walls &amp; fully insulate the floor above that crawlspace if it is indeed not intended to be a heated crawlspace. This is not a fun job, however required if you want to significantly reduce your heating bills &amp; increase your comfort in the house.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Using Biomass - Wood Stove &amp; Pellet Stove&lt;/b&gt;



Your house has 3 heating systems, that are all relatively new and include:



1. An ESTAR Trane XR95 (95% AFUE)  high efficiency condensing propane furnace - 73,000 BTU rated output. This furnace is installed in the basement of the right side addition &amp; has a very limited heat distribution ductwork system to the central part of the house. In fact, there are really no ducts supplying the upper floor bedrooms - just gravity feed through some access vent holes cut through the walls.



2. A Blaze King "King" wood stove - US EPA rated output of 39,800 BTU's @ 82% tested efficiency. The wood stove is installed in the basement &amp; depends upon the heat to rise up the basement stairwell &amp; distribute through the rest of the house. It depends on the same openings outlined above to provide heat into the upper bedroom areas.



3. A Drolet ECO 55 ST. wood pellet stove, with an output range of 6,648 - 28,540 BTU's and an efficiency of 75.8% at the higher heating level. The pellet stove is installed on the main floor of the central original part of the house to help heat the living room and left "entrance" side of the house.



NOTE: Based on the energy modeling I have performed for your house (including the assumption that the crawlspace is unheated and the floor above is insulated), the software calculates the design heat loss of your house at about 84,000 BTU's. This represents the peak heating load on the coldest day in January. Although none of your 3 heating systems can meet the full design heat load. the propane furnace &amp; wood stove could each (independently) do a reasonable job meeting the annual heating requirements of the home. The biggest issue you face is having adequate heat distribution to the various areas of your house.



You had mentioned that the 2017/18 heating season required approximately:

       - 7 cords of wood

       - 2.5 pallets of wood pellets

       - $800 of propane (for the furnace, hot water &amp; your gas stove)



It is obvious that you are primarily using the wood &amp; pellet stoves to heat your house.



For your information, this home energy evaluation rating is based on using the 95% AFUE propane furnace as the primary heat source - which is the system that can keep the house from freezing if you needed to go away for 2 weeks. I have then modeled the expected annual savings you could enjoy by using the wood stove &amp; pellet stove 100% of the time to help offset furnace use. The software calculates about $3,009 of annual savings .......... that you put towards the cost of havesting your own fire wood &amp; purchasing wood pellets.



I recommend you look to ways to more effectively distribute heat (from both your furnace &amp; the wood stove) throughout the rest of your house &amp; the furnace needs a better return air design (everything is funneled through a little 4" pipe). Gaining access to the crawlspace below the main central part of the house is critical to figuring out how well the heat distribution system is &amp; to determine the actual level of insulation in there (including insulation of the supply plenum itself) and to complete air sealing work in that space.



You should talk to your heating contractor regarding a better positioning of the return air supply to the furnace so you can use the circulation fan from that furnace to more evenly distribute the heat from the woodstove. That heating contractor also needs to become creative to look for better heat supply &amp; return air options for the upper bedroom level.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Ventilation&lt;/b&gt;



Your home currently has very little ventilation:



1. The kitchen range hood fan has relatively no section.

2. The 2 bathroom fans both do not work very well.

3. The dryer appears to vent to the outside, however created very little "suction" when I tested it with my blower door &amp; pressure gauges.



I would recommend you consider upgrading both bathroom fans to ENERGY STAR rated units connected to a humidistat, or a timer switch. The kitchen range hood fan should also be updated to a new Energy Star unit.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Depressurization Test&lt;/b&gt;



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the kitchen range hood fan, both bathroom fans and the dryer) indicated a pressure drop of only 0.3 Pascals. As this number is lower than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment should not cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current furnace is a direct vent model &amp; the hot water tank is power vented. The wood stove does have an 8" chimney &amp; the wood pellet stove has a 3" power vented chimney.



I did see 2 combustion air supply pipes - one is a 4" combustion air pot near the hot water tank &amp; the other is a vent in the floor near the wood pellet stove.



During my assessment of your home, there were both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors in the house. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Fort Simpson&lt;/b&gt;



The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Fort Simpson:

Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.31 per kWh up to the TPSP threshold, $0.70 per kWh thereafter

Oil  - $1.07 per litre 

Propane - $0.60 per litre

Wood - $367.50 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $7.35 per bag, $393.75 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



